comments[6]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-10-14T16:21:40.172Z","harness:codex","Duplicate check: strict2965-item all-status corpus; focused live extension invalid scalar boolean validation search; title/description scan for coercion and scalar-validation owners found only unrelated closed schema-field/numeric-wrapper work. Actual isolated echo reproduction on2026-10-14 returns exit0 and options.enabled=not-a-boolean. This pre-existing issue is separate from pm-gh1466 false preservation and pm-gh1467 optional arity; leave open/unclaimed and do not silently broaden those compatibility fixes."
  "2026-10-14T16:47:27.101Z","harness:codex","Discovery provenance: the malformed scalar echo was encountered while implementing pm-gh1466, so record discovered_from rather than a fabricated ordering/recurrence relationship. Risk, confidence, estimate and expected outcome are now complete. This separately queued enhancement remains open/unclaimed."
  "2026-10-15T12:29:34.130Z","harness:claude-code","Implementation note (2026-10-15): FlagDefinition gains value_validation (\"coerce\" | \"strict\"). The default stays the historical passthrough (compatible). strict refuses malformed number/boolean tokens before the extension handler runs with extension_flag_value_invalid (exit 2), naming flag, command path, expected grammar and received token; list entries are checked one by one; a value-less number flag is \"received no value\". Canonical switch presence and the legacy bool optional-value grammar are preserved: a switch followed by a positional resolves to true and keeps the positional in args. Registration refuses an unknown policy and refuses strict on string/untyped flags because there is nothing to validate; the activation failure surfaces in pm package doctor as extension_activate_failed. Shared SDK helper describeFlagScalarValueFailure is exported through runtime-primitives. Verified via a file-based extension fixture in a sandbox and through the public CLI in integration tests.\n"
  "2026-10-15T18:12:24.346Z","harness:claude-code","Review follow-up (PR #1479, CodeRabbit): strict scalar validation ran after alias folding, so '--limit 5 -l junk' kept the canonical 5 and silently discarded the malformed alias token. Every supplied spelling of a strict non-list scalar is now validated before mergeAliasOptionKeys discards it; a valid alias still yields to the canonical value. Covered in tests/unit/cli/main-loose-options.spec.ts."
  "2026-10-15T19:01:36.558Z","harness:claude-code","Review follow-up (PR #1479, CodeRabbit, third pass): two more strict-validation leaks closed. An attached token on a boolean switch (--verbose=maybe) was folded to presence true; the loose tokenizer now marks attached values and a malformed attached token refuses with extension_flag_value_invalid while a following positional keeps presence semantics. An explicitly empty list entry (--ids=, also for repeatable flags) was dropped by list normalization before validation and reached the handler as []; it is now refused before normalization. The coercion loop was split into coerceDefinedLooseOption and helpers to stay under the complexity ceiling, which released one eslint suppression (ceiling 106 to 105)."
  "2026-10-15T19:58:04.047Z","harness:claude-code","Review follow-up (PR #1479, CodeRabbit, fourth pass): on a strict CSV list, an empty segment inside one token (--ids=1,,2) was dropped by flattenFlagListValue before validation. refuseEmptyStrictListEntries now splits CSV occurrences on commas before the empty-entry check, while repeatable flags keep commas inside one occurrence so the whole token is reported as the malformed value. Covered in tests/unit/cli/main-loose-options.spec.ts."
